Fixed-step timing and determinism

This mechanic must use the game’s simulation dt.

Do not use:

* setTimeout;
* setInterval;
* Date.now;
* performance.now;
* render-frame timestamps;
* CSS/animation time;
* another wall clock.

The game is fixed-step and deterministic. Launch recovery must remain deterministic as well.

Follow the repository’s existing floating-point boundary convention, including its use of PHASE_EPSILON or the appropriate equivalent where needed.

The mechanic must work correctly when dt is not an exact divisor of a timing boundary.

It must also behave correctly if a call receives a dt larger than 0.5 seconds.

For example, if enough simulation time passes in one update for three complete recovery ticks, remove 3 points rather than only 1.

Do not silently lose elapsed complete intervals.

Always clamp:

launchPoint >= 0

Do not introduce fractional Launch Points. Recovery always removes whole points.

Interaction with fighter state

The recovery clock is based solely on simulation time since the last genuine hit.

It should not stop merely because the fighter is:

* airborne;
* grounded;
* attacking;
* in attack recovery;
* shielding;
* stunned;
* paralyzed;
* dashing;
* air dashing;
* using Combat Assist;
* otherwise unable to act.

Follow the same fixed-step timing conventions as Alva’s other passive combat timers.

If simulation steps themselves are paused, recovery naturally pauses because dt is not advancing.

Do not add a separate pause check solely for Launch recovery.

Hitstop / impact freeze

Respect the current simulation architecture.

Fighter.update(dt) advances combat timers using the existing fixed-step conventions even around impact-freeze handling. Launch recovery should follow that same convention rather than inventing wall-clock behavior or a special hitstop clock.

The hit itself resets the recovery timer, so any subsequent simulated time is measured from that hit.

Void, elimination, respawn and restart

Do not add a special out-of-play recovery loop.

A fighter that falls into the Void is removed from normal fighter updating while waiting to respawn. Preserve that architecture.

A fresh life already creates/resets combat state.

Required behavior:

* fresh fighter: Launch Point 0, fresh recovery timing;
* restart/rematch: Launch Point 0, fresh recovery timing;
* respawn after the Void: Launch Point 0, fresh recovery timing;
* while eliminated and waiting for respawn, do not invent background Launch Point recovery.

Do not change scoring, Void timing, or respawn timing.

No fighter-specific configuration

This is one universal rule.

Do not add recovery fields to js/data/characters/<id>.js.

